Fail when several targets are given and LINK is not a directory.
"ln a b c" with a c that is not a directory linked each target to c in
turn: the first link was made and the others failed with "File exists",
or with -f each replaced the one before. Check, as coreutils does, that
the last operand is a directory when there are more than two, before
making any link.

---
 coreutils/ln.c |    4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

--- a/coreutils/ln.c
+++ b/coreutils/ln.c
@@ -119,6 +119,10 @@
 		bb_simple_error_msg_and_die("-T accepts 2 args max");
 	}
 
+	if (argc > 2 && !is_directory(last, !(opts & LN_NODEREFERENCE))) {
+		bb_error_msg_and_die("target '%s' is not a directory", last);
+	}
+
 	if (!argv[1]) {
 		/* "ln PATH/TO/FILE" -> "ln PATH/TO/FILE FILE" */
 		*--argv = last;
